#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:65
msgid "Model Equations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:67
msgid ""
"Herein, we outline the equations for the \"one-state hysteresis\" model "
"(Axen, 2022), the \"one-state differential capacity hysteresis\" model "
"(Wycisk, 2022), and the \"current Sigmoid\" model (Ai et al., 2022). In this "
"notebook we collectively paraphrase all the original literature models into "
"a common notation and definition. For the original model expressions please "
"refer to the appropriate references."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:69
msgid "In all of the models, the open-circuit potential is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:71
msgid ""
"U = \\frac{1+h}{2} U_{\\text{delith}} + \\frac{1-h}{2} U_{\\text{lith}},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:73
msgid ""
"where :math:`h` is a hysteresis state variable, :math:`U_{\\text{delith}}` "
"is the delithiation open-circuit potential, and :math:`U_{\\text{lith}}` is "
"the lithiation open-circuit potential. The hysteresis state variable :math:"
"`h` is defined between -1 and 1, where 1 corresponds to the delithiation "
"branch and -1 corresponds to the lithiation branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:76
msgid "One-state hysteresis (Axen)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:78
msgid ""
"In this model, the state variable :math:`h(z,t)` is both stoichiometry and "
"time-dependent, and is governed by the following ODE:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:80
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:93
msgid ""
"\\frac{dh(z,t)}{dt} = \\gamma \\left( \\frac{i_{\\text{vol}}}{F \\, "
"c_{\\text{max}} \\, \\epsilon}\\right) \\frac{\\left(1 - \\text{sgn}"
"(i_{\\text{vol}}) \\, h(z,t)\\right)}{2},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:82
msgid ""
"where :math:`i_{\\text{vol}}` is the volumetric interfacial current "
"density, :math:`F` is the Faraday constant, :math:`c_{\\text{max}}` is the "
"maximum lithium concentration in the particles and :math:`\\epsilon` is the "
"active material volume fraction. In this model, the rate parameter :math:"
"`\\gamma` is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:84
msgid ""
"\\gamma = \\left( \\frac{1-\\text{sgn}(i_{\\text{vol}})}{2}  "
"\\gamma_{\\text{lith}} + \\frac{1+\\text{sgn}(i_{\\text{vol}})}{2} "
"\\gamma_{\\text{delith}}\\right),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:86
msgid ""
"which allows for a different decay rate during delithiation (:math:"
"`\\gamma_{\\text{delith}}`) and lithiation (:math:`\\gamma_{\\text{lith}}`). "
"Note that this implementation is a reformulation of the governing equations "
"in the original paper. Also note that :math:`\\gamma` is dimensionless."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:89
msgid "One-state differential capacity hysteresis (Wycisk)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:91
msgid ""
"As in the Axen model, the state variable :math:`h(z,t)` is both "
"stoichiometry and time-dependent, and is governed by the same ODE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:95
msgid "where now :math:`\\gamma(z)` is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:97
msgid ""
"\\gamma(z) = \\Gamma(z) \\cdot "
"\\left(\\frac{C_{\\text{diff}}\\left(z\\right)}{C_{\\text{ref,"
"vol}}}\\right)^{-x}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:99
msgid ""
"Here :math:`C_{\\text{diff}}(z)` is the differential capacity with respect "
"to potential, expressed as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:101
msgid ""
"C_{\\text{diff}}(z) = \\frac{dQ}{dU_{\\text{eq}}(z)},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:103
msgid ""
"where :math:`Q` is the capacity of the phase of active material experiencing "
"the voltage hysteresis and :math:`U_{\\text{eq}}` is the average open-"
"circuit potential of the phase of active material experiencing the voltage "
"hysteresis. The remaining parameters are :math:`\\Gamma` and :math:`x` which "
"are both fitting parameters that affect the response of the hysteresis state "
"decay when passing charge in either direction. The parameter :math:"
"`C_{\\text{ref,vol}}` is the reference differential capacity, which in this "
"implementation is hard-coded to 1 :math:`\\text{F}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:107
msgid "Current Sigmoid (Ai)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:109
msgid ""
"This approach uses a sigmoid function to switch between the delithiation and "
"lithiation branches of the open-circuit potential, depending on the sign of "
"the current. The original paper gives the following expression for the open-"
"circuit potential:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:111
msgid ""
"U = \\text{sigmoid}\\left(-\\frac{KI(t)}{Q_{\\text{cell}}}\\right) "
"U_{\\text{delith}} + \\text{sigmoid}\\left(\\frac{KI(t)}"
"{Q_{\\text{cell}}}\\right) U_{\\text{lith}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:115
msgid ""
"Where :math:`K` is a fitting parameter (in the current implementation this "
"is hard-coded to 100, as in the original publication), :math:`I` is the cell "
"current, and :math:`Q_{\\text{cell}}` is the cell capacity. To simplify the "
"comparison with the other models, we can rewrite this expression in terms of "
"the hysteresis state variable :math:`h` given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:117
msgid ""
"h(t) = 1 - 2 \\, \\text{sigmoid}\\left(-\\frac{KI(t)}"
"{Q_{\\text{cell}}}\\right).\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:129
msgid "Comparing the different hysteresis state models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:131
msgid ""
"We now compare the behavior of the different hysteresis state models. First "
"we define the models, which are all based on the DFN model with a composite "
"negative electrode comprised of two particles phases. One phase is modelled "
"using a single open-circuit potential, while the other is modelled using a "
"hysteresis state model. The positive electrode is modelled using a single "
"open-circuit potential."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[12]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"models = {\n"
"    \"current sigmoid\": pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN(\n"
"        {\n"
"            \"open-circuit potential\": ((\"single\", \"current sigmoid\"), "
"\"single\"),\n"
"            \"particle phases\": (\"2\", \"1\"),\n"
"            \"thermal\": \"lumped\",\n"
"        }\n"
"    ),\n"
"    \"one-state differential capacity hysteresis\": pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN(\n"
"        {\n"
"            \"open-circuit potential\": (\n"
"                (\"single\", \"one-state differential capacity "
"hysteresis\"),\n"
"                \"single\",\n"
"            ),\n"
"            \"particle phases\": (\"2\", \"1\"),\n"
"            \"thermal\": \"lumped\",\n"
"        }\n"
"    ),\n"
"    \"one-state hysteresis\": pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN(\n"
"        {\n"
"            \"open-circuit potential\": ((\"single\", \"one-state "
"hysteresis\"), \"single\"),\n"
"            \"particle phases\": (\"2\", \"1\"),\n"
"            \"thermal\": \"lumped\",\n"
"        }\n"
"    ),\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:177
msgid ""
"Next we define the parameter set, which is for a graphite and silicon "
"negative electrode with a NMC positive electrode. We add some thermal "
"parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[13]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"parameters = pybamm.ParameterValues(\"Chen2020_composite\")\n"
"parameters.update(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Negative current collector density [kg.m-3]\": 8933.0,\n"
"        \"Negative current collector specific heat capacity [J.kg-1.K-1]\": "
"385.0,\n"
"        \"Negative current collector thermal conductivity [W.m-1.K-1]\": "
"398.0,\n"
"        \"Negative electrode density [kg.m-3]\": 1555.0,\n"
"        \"Negative electrode specific heat capacity [J.kg-1.K-1]\": 1437.0,\n"
"        \"Negative electrode thermal conductivity [W.m-1.K-1]\": 1.58,\n"
"        \"Separator density [kg.m-3]\": 1017.0,\n"
"        \"Separator specific heat capacity [J.kg-1.K-1]\": 1978.0,\n"
"        \"Separator thermal conductivity [W.m-1.K-1]\": 0.34,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode density [kg.m-3]\": 3699.0,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode specific heat capacity [J.kg-1.K-1]\": 1270.0,\n"
"        \"Positive electrode thermal conductivity [W.m-1.K-1]\": 1.04,\n"
"        \"Positive current collector density [kg.m-3]\": 2702.0,\n"
"        \"Positive current collector specific heat capacity [J.kg-1.K-1]\": "
"903.0,\n"
"        \"Positive current collector thermal conductivity [W.m-1.K-1]\": "
"238.0,\n"
"        \"Total heat transfer coefficient [W.m-2.K-1]\": 5.0,\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:218
msgid ""
"Next, we need to add the additional parameters required by the model. In "
"this example, :math:`K_{\\text{lith}} = K_{\\text{delith}} = 100` for the HS "
"model, but it is recommended to test different values for the decay rate "
"aiming for good agreement to experiment if the information is available, or "
"a smooth transition between the lithiation and delithiation branches of the "
"hysteresis. For example, depending on the case study, an acceptable value "
"for :math:`K_{\\text{lith}}` and :math:`K_{\\text{delith}}` could be :math:"
"`10` or lower."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[15]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# Wycisk parameters\n"
"parameters_wycisk = parameters.copy()\n"
"parameters_wycisk.update(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Secondary: Negative particle hysteresis decay rate\": 0.1,\n"
"        \"Secondary: Negative particle hysteresis switching factor\": 10,\n"
"        \"Secondary: Initial hysteresis state in negative electrode\": 1,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# Axen parameters\n"
"parameters_axen = parameters.copy()\n"
"parameters_axen.update(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Secondary: Negative particle lithiation hysteresis decay rate\": "
"100,\n"
"        \"Secondary: Negative particle delithiation hysteresis decay rate\": "
"100,\n"
"        \"Secondary: Initial hysteresis state in negative electrode\": 1,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"parameter_values = {\n"
"    \"current sigmoid\": parameters,\n"
"    \"one-state differential capacity hysteresis\": parameters_wycisk,\n"
"    \"one-state hysteresis\": parameters_axen,\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:264
msgid ""
"Then we define the experiment, which is a discharge and charge cycle at 1C."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[16]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"experiment = pybamm.Experiment(\n"
"    [\n"
"        (\n"
"            \"Discharge at 1C for 1 hour or until 2.5 V\",\n"
"            \"Rest for 15 minutes\",\n"
"            \"Charge at 1C until 4.2 V\",\n"
"            \"Hold at 4.2 V until 0.05 C\",\n"
"            \"Rest for 15 minutes\",\n"
"        ),\n"
"    ]\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:295
msgid "We then loop over the models and solve them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[17]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solutions = {}\n"
"for name, model in models.items():\n"
"    sim = pybamm.Simulation(\n"
"        model, experiment=experiment, "
"parameter_values=parameter_values[name]\n"
"    )\n"
"    solutions[name] = sim.solve(calc_esoh=False)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:321
msgid "Finally, we plot the results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[18]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/hysteresis-state-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"output_variables = [\n"
"    \"Current [A]\",\n"
"    \"Negative electrode secondary stoichiometry\",\n"
"    \"Voltage [V]\",\n"
"    \"Volume-averaged cell temperature [K]\",\n"
"    \"Total heating [W.m-3]\",\n"
"    \"Hysteresis electrochemical heating [W.m-3]\",\n"
"    \"X-averaged negative electrode secondary hysteresis state\",\n"
"    \"X-averaged negative electrode secondary open-circuit potential [V]\",\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"pybamm.dynamic_plot(\n"
"    list(solutions.values()),\n"
"    labels=list(solutions.keys()),\n"
"    colors=[\"black\", \"blue\", \"red\"],\n"
"    linestyles=[\"-\", \"--\", \"-.\"],\n"
"    output_variables=output_variables,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

